{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310447,"nonce":"zwnKWbA6hOH0zG+kzJFZerh36gg=","title":"Generated wire JavaScript passes a left-out optional argument as an explicit undefined","message":"In the browser, WebGPU's `setBindGroup` stopped working after the page had run for a while, and the draw after it failed with \"No bind group set at group index 0\".\n\n**What happens**\n\nThe generated op decoder reads an optional argument as `r.u8() ? value : undefined` and then calls `self.method(a0, a1, a2)` whatever `a2` is. For `GPURenderPassEncoder.setBindGroup(index, group, dynamicOffsets)` that is `setBindGroup(0, group, undefined)`. `setBindGroup` is overloaded, and in Chrome that call worked for the first dozens of frames and then threw \"The provided value cannot be converted to a sequence\" on every call (550 times in one run), so no group was bound and the next draw was rejected.\n\n**Seen in**\n\nashui's Banner scene running in headless Chrome through Ash's wasm target, with xgpu's agent (gpu-agent.mjs from hlwgpu's build): the program ran 14 to 28 seconds, depending on frame rate, and then died on the first draw error. With the third argument left off, the same page ran for a minute and more, still drawing.\n\n**Fix**\n\nLeave the trailing optional arguments that are undefined off the call, so `a2 === undefined ? self.setBindGroup(a0, a1) : self.setBindGroup(a0, a1, a2)`. The test generates the JavaScript for a method with one and with two trailing optional arguments and runs it under Node against a stand-in that counts its arguments.\n\n**Downstream**\n\nxgpu pins x-idl, hlwgpu pins xgpu, and the agent in a page comes from hlwgpu's build, so each pin needs a bump before a page gets the fix.","files":null}]}